Hecks.bluebook "InboxIndex", version: "2026.05.20.1" do
  vision "The end of the inbox-number-collision pattern. Tonight (2026-05-20) two pairs of agents collided on the same i-number (i649 : audit vs autophagy ; i656 : universal-door vs executable-bluebook) — each agent independently scanned its own worktree's `hecks_conception/inbox/`, picked `max(i*.md) + 1`, and filed simultaneously. Per-worktree max is a local view of a global resource ; concurrent local-max picks collide. The fix is a centralized atomic allocator. InboxIndex is the singleton substrate that owns the global next-number ; Allocate is the atomic increment ; Reserve takes a contiguous range for a sidequest with N expected cards ; Recover re-scans every project inbox on disk and reclaims missing numbers (so the allocator stays consistent if a card is deleted or a hand-edit happens). The persistent ground-truth is `~/.hecks/inbox/next.json` (outside any worktree, the same shape `~/.hecks/inbox/` already hosts the broader inbox state), read + atomically updated through a flock-guarded shim at `bin/hecks-inbox`. Agents call the shim before filing ; the SessionStart hook injects the current next-number into additionalContext so an agent sees it inline at boot."
  category "framework"

  # ============================================================
  # INBOX INDEX — the centralized i-number allocator
  # ============================================================
  #
  # Filed 2026-05-20. Chris : "inboxes should pull data from ~/hecks
  # so we don't collide."
  #
  # ── The collision pattern (the bug this domain ends) ──
  #
  # Inbox cards in `hecks_conception/inbox/` are named `i<N>.md` or
  # `i<N>-<slug>.md`. Today, an agent filing a card scans its OWN
  # worktree's inbox dir, finds `max(i*.md) + 1`, and writes. When
  # two agents in two worktrees do this concurrently — both pick
  # the same N. Tonight that produced two pairs : i649 and i656.
  #
  # The root cause is structural : per-worktree max is a LOCAL view
  # of a GLOBAL resource. The fix is to move the resource out of the
  # worktree. `~/.hecks/inbox/next.json` is the single source of
  # truth ; the allocator atomically reads-increments-writes it
  # under flock so concurrent allocators serialize.
  #
  # ── Two operations, one aggregate ──
  #
  #   * Allocate — return the current next_number AND advance it
  #     by 1, in one atomic step. The default verb for "I'm about
  #     to file a card, give me a number."
  #
  #   * Reserve — return a contiguous range [start..start+count-1]
  #     AND advance next_number by count, in one atomic step. The
  #     verb for "I'm spawning N sidequest agents that will each
  #     file a card — give me a block so they don't collide with
  #     each other or with anyone else."
  #
  #   * Recover — re-scan every known project inbox directory,
  #     compute the global max(i*.md), set next_number to max+1.
  #     Idempotent — running it never decreases next_number, only
  #     advances it if disk reality has overtaken state. Run by
  #     hand when a hand-edit or rename creates suspicion that
  #     state has drifted ; run automatically on first call (init).
  #
  # ── Persistence : ~/.hecks/inbox/next.json ──
  #
  # The aggregate's state persists outside any worktree, at
  # `~/.hecks/inbox/next.json`. Shape :
  #
  #     {
  #       "next_number":         658,
  #       "last_allocated_at":   "2026-05-20T22:14:03Z",
  #       "last_allocated_to":   "inbox-centralized-numbering",
  #       "total_allocated":     12,
  #       "total_reserved":      3,
  #       "recoveries":          1
  #     }
  #
  # The bin/hecks-inbox shim implements the atomicity via `flock`
  # on the file itself : read → increment → write under exclusive
  # lock so concurrent callers serialize at the OS level. The
  # bluebook IS the contract ; the shim is the impure execution.
  #
  # ── Scope discipline ──
  #
  # InboxIndex allocates NUMBERS. It does not write cards, route
  # cards to inboxes, or know about card content. Filing is still
  # the agent's act — InboxIndex just hands out the next number,
  # atomically, on demand. The agent then writes
  # `i<N>[-slug].md` into the appropriate project inbox.
  #
  # The scan in Recover is broad on purpose : every `inbox/` and
  # `*.inbox/` directory under `~/Projects/*/`, every
  # `hecks_conception/inbox/` across worktrees. The collisions
  # we're ending happened because agents only saw their own
  # worktree ; Recover is the catch-up sweep that re-grounds
  # state against ALL of disk.

  aggregate "InboxIndex", "Singleton substrate for centralized i-number allocation. Owns next_number, the single source of truth for what the next inbox card's i-number should be. Allocate atomically returns + increments ; Reserve takes a contiguous range ; Recover re-scans disk to reclaim drifted state. Effectively a singleton — there is one inbox-number namespace per operator. Conventional `identified_by :name` shape for now ; under i611 phase 1 (when `singleton: true` lands) this converts." do
    identified_by :name

    attribute :name, Name

    # The number that will be returned by the NEXT Allocate call.
    # Advanced by 1 on Allocate, by N on Reserve(count: N), and
    # re-grounded to max(disk) + 1 on Recover.
    attribute :next_number, Number

    # Session metadata
    attribute :session_started_at, Timestamp

    # Last-operation surface for the statusline / quick reads.
    attribute :last_allocated_at, Timestamp
    attribute :last_allocated_to, Requester
    attribute :last_reserved_at,  Timestamp
    attribute :last_recovered_at, Timestamp

    # Lifetime tallies (per-session resets via StartSession).
    attribute :total_allocated, AllocationCount
    attribute :total_reserved,  ReservationCount
    attribute :recoveries,      RecoveryCount

    # ── Value-objects : the language of allocation ──

    value_object "Name" do
      # Singleton key — there is exactly one InboxIndex per operator.
      # Under i611 phase 1 (`singleton: true`), this VO disappears.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Number" do
      # An i-number. Form : a positive integer. Cards are filed as
      # `i<value>.md` or `i<value>-<slug>.md`. Allocate returns one ;
      # Reserve returns a range of these.
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "Timestamp" do
      # ISO-8601 UTC timestamp. Recorded on every Allocate / Reserve /
      # Recover so the audit surface answers "who got what when."
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Requester" do
      # Free-form label naming the agent or branch that requested an
      # allocation. Recorded verbatim — no validation of the form.
      # Examples : "inbox-centralized-numbering" (a branch name),
      # "audit-sidequest" (an agent task tag), "manual" (Chris filing
      # a card by hand). The audit surface, not a foreign key.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    value_object "Range" do
      # A contiguous allocation result from Reserve. start is inclusive,
      # count is the size. The reserved numbers are [start, start+count-1].
      # Reserve advances next_number by count atomically, so callers can
      # use the range without checking for further collision.
      attribute :start, Integer
      attribute :count, Integer
    end

    value_object "AllocationCount" do
      # Lifetime count of Allocate dispatches in the current session.
      # Reset on StartSession ; the durable disk file keeps cross-session
      # totals if reporting needs them.
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "ReservationCount" do
      # Lifetime count of Reserve dispatches in the current session.
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "RecoveryCount" do
      # Lifetime count of Recover dispatches in the current session.
      # Recovery is expected to be rare — running it under normal
      # operation is a no-op (next_number already exceeds disk max).
      attribute :value, Integer
    end

    value_object "ScanRoot" do
      # A directory the Recover scan walks. The Recover command takes
      # a list of these so the operator (or the bin/hecks-inbox shim)
      # can scope the scan precisely. Default scan roots are documented
      # in the shim ; the bluebook stays declarative about what a
      # ScanRoot is, not what the defaults are.
      attribute :value, String
    end

    # ── Session lifecycle ──

    command "StartSession" do
      role "System"
      description "Reset per-session counters at runtime boot. Lifetime totals on disk (in next.json) are preserved — this resets only the in-memory tallies the aggregate exposes for observability."
      attribute :session_started_at, Timestamp
      then_set :session_started_at, to: :session_started_at
      emits "SessionStarted"
    end

    # ── Primary operation 1 : Allocate ──

    command "Allocate" do
      role "Agent"
      description "Atomically return the current next_number and advance it by 1. The default verb for 'I'm about to file an inbox card ; give me the next i-number.' Atomicity is enforced by the bin/hecks-inbox shim's flock on the persistence file — concurrent Allocate dispatches from sibling agents serialize at the OS level, so each gets a distinct number. The returned number is what the caller writes as `i<N>.md` into the target inbox ; InboxIndex does not file the card or know which inbox it lands in. requester is a free-form label (branch, task tag, 'manual') recorded for the audit surface. Increments total_allocated ; updates last_allocated_at and last_allocated_to ; emits NumberAllocated so downstream observers (the statusline counter, an audit reactor) can react."
      attribute :allocated_number, Number
      attribute :requester,        Requester
      attribute :allocated_at,     Timestamp
      emits "NumberAllocated"
      then_set :last_allocated_at, to: :allocated_at
      then_set :last_allocated_to, to: :requester
    end

    # ── Primary operation 2 : Reserve ──

    command "Reserve" do
      role "Agent"
      description "Atomically take a contiguous range of N i-numbers and advance next_number by N. The verb for spawning sidequest agents : 'I'm dispatching 5 child agents who will each file a card ; reserve a block of 5 so they can pick from a known range without colliding with each other or with anyone else.' Returned as a Range value object (start + count). The caller is responsible for distributing the range to its children ; InboxIndex just hands out the block atomically. Increments total_reserved ; updates last_reserved_at ; emits RangeReserved."
      attribute :reserved_range, Range
      attribute :requester,      Requester
      attribute :reserved_at,    Timestamp
      emits "RangeReserved"
      then_set :last_reserved_at, to: :reserved_at
      then_set :last_allocated_to, to: :requester
    end

    # ── Primary operation 3 : Recover ──

    command "Recover" do
      role "System"
      description "Re-scan a set of inbox directories on disk, compute the global max(i*.md), and re-ground next_number to max + 1. Idempotent — never decreases next_number, only advances it if disk reality has overtaken the persisted state (e.g. a hand-filed card with a guessed number, a worktree merge that brought in cards filed elsewhere, a rename that exposed a higher number than the allocator knew about). Run automatically on the first call to Allocate when next.json does not yet exist ; run by hand via `hecks-inbox recover` when the operator suspects drift. The scan_roots attribute carries the list of directories walked — the bin/hecks-inbox shim defaults this to the union of all `~/Projects/*/inbox/`, `~/Projects/*/*.inbox/`, and `~/Projects/*/.../hecks_conception/inbox/` directories the scan finds. Increments recoveries ; updates last_recovered_at ; emits StateRecovered carrying the max observed on disk and whether next_number was advanced."
      attribute :observed_max,    Number
      attribute :previous_next,   Number
      attribute :new_next,        Number
      attribute :recovered_at,    Timestamp
      emits "StateRecovered"
      then_set :next_number,      to: :new_next
      then_set :last_recovered_at, to: :recovered_at
    end
  end
end
